//Suppose we have a logical argument with the following premises and conclusion:

    //Premises: p ∨ ¬q, r → q, r ∨ q
    //Conclusion: p ∧ q

//Use a truth table to prove that the argument is valid. You should get the message "Logika verified"
//when you finish.

//After your truth table(s), write a brief explanation of why you know
//from your truth tables that the argument is valid.

//PUT YOUR EXPLANATION HERE (start each line with //)

//The argument is valid because every truth assignment that made all the premses true 
//also made the conclusion true. The truth assignments [T T T] and [T T F] (for [p q r])
//were the only ones that made each premise true, and they also made the conclusion true.




//PUT YOUR TRUTH TABLE BELOW THIS LINE

           *       *        *         *
------------------------------------------
p q r # (p | !q, r ->: q, r | q) |- p & q
------------------------------------------
T T T #    T F     T        T         T
T T F #    T F     T        T         T
T F T #    T T     F        T         F
T F F #    T T     T        F         F
F T T #    F F     T        T         F
F T F #    F F     T        T         F
F F T #    T T     F        T         F
F F F #    T T     T        F         F
--------------------------------------------

Valid [T T T] [T T F]